  <h2>What are the four conditions that force real capture?</h2>

  <p>These are not preferences. Each one has something behind it: a physical limit, a marketplace rule, or a statute.</p>

  <p><strong>Material truth.</strong> Anything whose selling point is how a surface behaves. Fabric weave, brushed metal, glass, liquid, anything transparent or reflective. This is the one place where the limitation is measurable rather than aesthetic.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Physical realism, not semantic accuracy, is where current image generation still breaks: a 900-case benchmark covering optics, mechanics and state transition scored eleven current image editing models against human-annotated reflection surfaces and contact points, and every open-source model scored below 60, with only two closed systems slightly above it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: PICABench, image editing physical-realism benchmark, October 2025.</p>

  <p>There is a structural reason underneath the scores. A generated image is perceptually convincing without being physically plausible, because it never originated from a three-dimensional scene and so cannot be tied back to physical parameters, a point made in the Journal of Vision in March 2026 on the basis of tests run against earlier generators. Read that for the structure rather than as a verdict on this quarter's models. It holds either way. An image that never came from a scene has nothing to be checked against.</p>

  <p><strong>Regulated categories.</strong> Where the law or the platform says the picture has to be the product. Marketplaces moved first and moved specifically.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese marketplace became the first to publish a rule aimed specifically at AI fake product images, naming four failure categories: product material or style that does not match the real item, distorted or exaggerated product effects, an obvious cutout and paste look, and bodies or scenes that break physical laws.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Economic Information Daily, March 2025, reporting the platform's own rule announcement and its detection-model enforcement.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A general marketplace's seller image guidelines state that images generated by artificial intelligence must be truthful, accurate and not misleading, and that sellers must use actual product images that accurately reflect the product being sold.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: marketplace seller image guidelines, page dated May 2026, the platform's own published rules.</p>

  <p>Advertising regulators are enforcing the same principle without needing a special AI rule.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A UK advertising regulator upheld 22 complaints against an ad for a robot toy, finding that footage which appeared to show real or AI-generated puppies gave the impression the product was highly realistic, and ruled that the advertiser must not misleadingly represent or exaggerate the functionality and appearance of its products.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator ruling, 25 March 2026, an upheld investigation under the code's misleading advertising, substantiation and exaggeration rules.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same regulator's guidance holds that the advertising code is media-neutral: the rules apply regardless of how the content was created, and the advertiser remains responsible for any AI output used in its ads.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator guidance, June 2026.</p>

  <p>Media-neutral is the sentence to take away. Nobody is going to ask how the image was made. They are going to ask whether it overstated the product.</p>

  <p>Food and cosmetics carry their own version of the same duty, and both predate the AI question entirely.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">EU food law bars misleading a consumer by suggesting, through the appearance, the description or pictorial representations, the presence of an ingredient that has in fact been substituted, and applies the same rule to the advertising and presentation of the food, including its shape and appearance.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Regulation (EU) No 1169/2011, Article 7(1)(d) and Article 7(4), adopted 25 October 2011, as summarized on the European Commission's own fair information practices page.</p>

  <p>EU cosmetics rules do something similar through their honesty criterion, where a presentation of a product's performance may not go beyond the supporting evidence, under Commission Regulation (EU) No 655/2013, in application since July 2013.</p>

  <p><strong>Talent.</strong> Any real person, and increasingly any person-shaped asset at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A New York statute signed in December 2025 and in force since 9 June 2026 requires an advertisement using a synthetic performer to conspicuously disclose it, defining a synthetic performer as a digitally created asset intended to create the impression of a visual performance by a human performer who is not any identifiable real person.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: New York Senate Bill S8420A, signed 11 December 2025, effective 180 days later.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">EU transparency obligations for synthetic content apply from 2 August 2026, with a limited grace period to 2 December 2026 for marking obligations on systems already on the market, and require deployers of deep fakes to disclose them on first exposure in a clear and distinguishable manner.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: European Commission FAQ on Article 50 transparency obligations, page last updated 24 July 2026.</p>

  <p>Generating a person does not remove the paperwork. It changes which paperwork.</p>

  <p><strong>Physical scale.</strong> Vehicles, architecture, furniture in a real room, anything where the viewer's sense of size comes from cues a generator has no reason to respect. This is the softest of the four and the one that moves fastest, so test it per category rather than assuming.</p>

  <h2>What should you generate instead?</h2>

  <p>Everything that repeats. Colorways of an item already shot, environments around a fixed hero, crops for six platforms, localized set dressing, background extension, the forty-third lifestyle scene for a product you photographed properly once.</p>

  <p>The economics run backwards from how people usually describe them. The expensive part of a shoot is not the shutter. It is scheduling, sample logistics, styling, the crew day, and the reshoot when a sample turns up in the wrong finish. Generation removes almost none of the cost of the first image and almost all of the cost of the two hundredth.</p>

