  <p>Read down the "why" column rather than the "default" column. The defaults will move. The reasons will not, because three of the four conditions are written into rules rather than into model capability.</p>

  <h2>What goes wrong in each direction?</h2>

  <p>Generating what you should have shot fails publicly and slowly. The image passes internal review because it looks right on a monitor, then returns data or a complaint tells you the product did not match the picture. The regulatory version of that failure is the ruling above.</p>

  <p>Shooting what you should have generated fails privately and constantly, and it looks like a calendar problem rather than a creative one. The catalog ships late because eight hundred variants queued behind a studio day. A market gets skipped because a reshoot could not be scheduled. Nobody writes that up as a failure. It shows up as a slower launch every season.</p>

  <p>The asymmetry is worth naming. Over-generating produces a small number of severe failures. Over-shooting produces a large number of invisible ones. Teams reliably fear the first and absorb the second, which is how catalogs end up costing more than anyone budgeted for a reason nobody can point at.</p>

  <p>The cost of getting it wrong sits in rework, not in the split itself. A hero generated when it should have been captured has usually propagated into every variant derived from it by the time anyone notices, so the fix is the anchor plus everything downstream. A variant shot when it could have been generated costs a studio slot that something else needed, normally the hero of a different product. Both errors push work later in the calendar, which is where it gets expensive.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service page</a> sets out how the catalog split is scoped in practice, the <a href="/solutions/ai-production/image">AI image production page</a> covers the generation side, and the <a href="/services/design/video-production">video production service page</a> covers capture for anything moving.</p>

  <h2>Questions teams ask about shooting against generating</h2>

  <h3>When should a product be photographed instead of generated?</h3>

  <p>When the asset has to carry material truth, when a marketplace or a regulator requires the actual product, when a real person is in it, or when physical scale is the message. Those four conditions cover most of what still belongs in front of a camera. Everything else is a judgment call about volume.</p>

  <h3>What can current image generation not do convincingly?</h3>

  <p>Physical behavior rather than subject matter. An October 2025 benchmark covering optics, mechanics and state transitions scored eleven current editing models against human-annotated reflection surfaces and contact points, and every open-source model scored below 60. Reflections, contact shadows and material response are where it shows.</p>

  <h3>Do marketplaces allow AI-generated product images?</h3>

  <p>It varies, and the direction is toward requiring the image to represent the real item. One Chinese marketplace published a rule in March 2025 naming four AI failure categories including mismatched material and exaggerated effects. A general marketplace's published seller guidelines require AI images to be truthful and accurate and require sellers to use actual product images. Check the specific platform rules before a catalog runs.</p>

  <h3>Does an advertising regulator treat AI imagery differently?</h3>

  <p>Not as a separate category. UK regulator guidance from June 2026 states the advertising code is media-neutral: the rules apply regardless of how content was created, and the advertiser remains responsible for AI output used in its ads. In March 2026 the same regulator upheld 22 complaints where footage gave a misleading impression of how realistic a product was.</p>

  <h3>Do I need a disclosure for a generated model or presenter?</h3>

  <p>In some markets, yes. A New York statute in force since 9 June 2026 requires an advertisement using a synthetic performer to conspicuously disclose it. EU transparency obligations for synthetic content apply from 2 August 2026 and require deployers of deep fakes to disclose on first exposure. Treat a generated presenter as a paperwork question, not a shortcut around one.</p>

  <h3>What is a sensible starting ratio?</h3>

  <p>Most catalogs land near 70 percent generated once a proper set of anchors exists, but the ratio is an output, not a target. Work out which assets meet the four conditions, shoot those, generate what derives from them, and let the percentage fall where it falls. A team that starts from the percentage ends up arguing about the wrong thing.</p>
